es long, which rises in eastern Turkey and joins the Kura in Azerbaijan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aratta"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aratta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aratta</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Aratta is a distant land rich in precious stones and metals described in Sumerian epic poems, notably Enmerkar and the Lord of Aratta, in which Enmerkar, king of Uruk…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aratus of Sicyon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aratus of Sicyon was a Greek statesman who freed his city from its tyrant in 251 BCE, brought it into the Achaean League and led the league for over thirty years as its…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-soli"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aratus-of-soli.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aratus of Soli</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aratus of Soli was a Greek poet from Cilicia who lived at the Macedonian court of Antigonus II Gonatas.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arau</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arau is the royal town of Perlis, the smallest state of Malaysia, near the Thai border.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arauco-war"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arauco-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arauco War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1536–1818</span><span class="enc-snip">The Arauco War was the long conflict between the Spanish and the Mapuche of south-central Chile.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aravidu-dynasty"><span class="enc-name">Aravidu dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aravidu dynasty was the fourth and last ruling house of the Vijayanagara Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arawakan-languages"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arawakan-languages.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arawakan languages</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Arawakan languages are a family of languages of South America and the Caribbean, spoken from the Andes and Amazonia to the Antilles.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arbed"><span class="enc-name">ARBED</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">ARBED was a Luxembourg steel company formed in 1911 by the merger of several ironworks and steelworks.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arboga"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arboga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arboga</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Arboga is a small town in Västmanland in central Sweden, a medieval trading centre for the iron of the Bergslagen mining district.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/arbogast"><span class="enc-name">Arbogast</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Arbogast was a Frankish general in the Roman army who became the dominant figure at the western court of the young emperor Valentinian II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arbore-church"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/arbore-church.jpg" alt=